  "title": "Predicting Types of Nuclear Decay",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Isotopes that are not in the band of stability undergo radioactive decay. The type of decay for a given nuclide depends on its neutron to proton (n:p) ratio relative to the band of stability and the size of the nucleus. Nuclei with a higher n:p ratio than stable nuclei tend to undergo "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "β"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay. Emitting a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "β"
              },
              {
                "text": " particle decreases the number of neutrons and increases the number of protons, both of which decrease the n:p ratio. Nuclei with a lower n:p ratio than stable nuclei tend to undergo electron capture or positron emission. Both of these processes increase the number of neutrons and decrease the number of protons, therefore increasing the n:p ratio. Positron emission occurs more often than electron capture, and choosing between these two competing processes requires detailed calculations. As the size of the nucleus increases, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "α"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay becomes increasingly likely. Alpha decay reduces the size of the nucleus due to the loss of two protons and two neutrons. It is the most likely decay mode for nuclides with an atomic number over 82 and also occurs for somewhat lighter nuclei with a very low n:p ratio."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ep1uk5lru77xs0z",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Graph showing nuclear decay modes of known nuclides. "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "bb3d8b7c8e6c4ac3ba55d57d1f27ffff",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
