      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "77"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Br and "
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "85"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Br are two of the radioactive isotopes of the element bromine. Predict the type of radioactive decay each isotope is likely to undergo."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "qtlhdqopa1zqlhc",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The atomic mass of Br is 79.90 amu, so stable isotopes of Br are likely to have a mass number of approximately 80 amu and consist of 35 protons and (about) 45 neutrons. Its neutron to proton ratio is "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\frac{45}{35}\\;=\\;1.29",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "gn0q344xk3u3ork",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{85}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " consists of 35 protons and 50 neutrons, so it lies above the band of stability and has a higher n:p ratio ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\frac{50}{35}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 1.43) than stable isotopes of Br. If "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{85}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " undergoes "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "β"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " decay ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{85}\\mathrm{Br}\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{}_{-1}^0{}\\mathrm e\\;+\\;{}_{36}^{85}\\mathrm{Kr}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "), the resulting nuclide is "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{36}^{85}\\mathrm{Kr}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". This isotope consists of 36 protons and 49 neutrons and is closer to a stable n/p ratio ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\frac{49}{36}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 1.36), so "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{85}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is likely to undergo "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "β"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " decay. (Note: "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{85}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " undergoes "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "β"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " decay, and the daughter nuclide "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{36}^{85}\\mathrm{Kr}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " undergoes "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "β"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " decay as well. The resulting "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{37}^{85}\\mathrm{Rb}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " isotope is stable.) "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "e4c4ff4e578340c3a78dda920712d28e",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{77}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " consists of 35 protons and 42 neutrons. It has a lower n:p ratio ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\frac{43}{35}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 1.17) than stable isotopes of Br and lies below the band of stability. If "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{77}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " undergoes positron emission or electron capture ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{77}\\mathrm{Br}\\;+\\;{}_{-1}^{\\;0}\\mathrm e\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{}_{34}^{77}\\mathrm{Se}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "), the resulting nuclide is "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{34}^{77}\\mathrm{Se}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". This isotope consists of 34 protons and 43 neutrons ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\frac{43}{34}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 1.26) and is closer to the stable n:p ratio, so "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{77}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is likely to undergo either positron emission or electron capture. (Note: "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{35}^{77}\\mathrm{Br}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " actually undergoes electron capture, and the daughter nuclide "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{34}^{77}\\mathrm{Se}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is stable.) "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "baeba48f15334b879df894a08454abf3",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
